erasure via value-space projection. To study erasure robustness, we adopt three attacks: Circumventing Concept Erasure (CCE) [ 36 ] , UnlearnDiff (UD) [ 65 ] and Ring-A-Bell (RAB) [ 57 ] . RAB (primarily designed for nudity concept erasure) and UD are prompt-based attacks, whereas CCE is an inversion-based attack that optimizes continuous text embeddings, yielding a larger and more flexible search space for recovering erased concepts. Evaluation Metrics: We evaluate concept erasure along two axes: erasure effectiveness and model utility preservation. To study erasure effectiveness, we evaluate Attack Success Rate - ASR [ 7 ] (lower is better) under (i) target: direct target prompts, (ii) adversarial attacks using CCE [ 36 ] , UD [ 65 ] , and RAB [ 56 ] , (iii) neighboring concept attack using SEE benchmark [ 40 ] , (iv) Paraphrases attack using EraseBench [ 1 ] . To study model utility preservation, we evaluate FID [ 18 ] (lower is better) and CLIP-score (higher is better) on non-target prompts, following RACE [ 23 , 52 ] . Together, ASR under attack measures robustness to re-emergence, while FID/CLIP quantify model utility preservation on benign prompts. To study balance between erasure robustness and model utility preservation, we report BEUS . Figure 3 : Robustness under attacks after nudity erasure, and utility on a benign retain prompt (“A photo of a person”). Many CETs reintroduce nudity under attack and over-suppress and fail to generate a person; PARSE stays robust (person with no nudity). Datasets, Oracles, and Prompts: Our concept erasure experiments span across three categories: (i) Nudity (NSFW) Removal (dataset: I2P dataset [ 49 ] , oracle: NudeNet [ 4 , 56 ] ), (ii) Artistic Style Removal (dataset: UD (“Van Gogh”) [ 65 ] , oracle: style classifier proposed by UD [ 35 , 65 ] , prompt: “A painting in the style of Van Gogh” [ 52 ] ), (iii) Object Removal (dataset: Imagenette classes [ 35 , 13 , 10 ] , oracle: ImageNet classifier (e.g., ResNet-50 [ 17 ] ), prompt: “A photo of a <object>” [ 52 ] ). To study post erasure model utility preservation, we generate 5000 images with captions from MS-COCO [ 30 ] validation set and compute FID [ 18 ] to measure distribution shift with unedited (SD 1.4) model and CLIP-score [ 37 ] to measure prompt-image alignment. Implementation Details: We use SD 1.4 as the pretrained base model for concept erasure. For iterative expansion, we run at most N = 5 N{=}5 iterations with early stopping when the trigger score falls below η = 0.6 \eta{=}0.6 . Textual inversion (TI) [ 12 ] attacks are trained for 1000 1000 optimization steps with learning rate 2 × 10 − 6 2	imes 10^{-6} . Computing the vocabulary CFG steering embeddings and image cache is a one-time, reusable cost of ≈ \approx 4.9 h on 2 × 4 2{	imes}4 H100 GPUs ( ≈ \approx 2.6 GB). Given a target, PARSE computes one target CFG embedding and ranks the stored index for the top- K K candidates in ≈ \approx 7 s ( ≈ \approx 4 GB), and online erasure with adaptive subspace expansion completes in 10.7 min on a single H100. Figure 4 : Left: Robustness under CCE/UD attacks after erasing “Van Gogh”, and utility on a non-target retain prompt (“Charming street scene in Kelly McKernan style”). Many CETs degrade non-target styles; PARSE removes the target style while preserving utility. Right: Illustration of erasing “garbage truck”, and utility on retain prompt (“A close-up photo of a tow truck”). Many CETs suppress adjacent concepts or reintroduce the target; PARSE both blocks target re-emergence while still generating the tow truck. 5.2 Experiment Results Concept Erasure Effectiveness: In Tab. 1 , we compare PARSE with diverse CETs across three categories: NSFW, art styles, and objects. We measure effectiveness by target ASR (lower is better). PARSE achieves state-of-the-art ASR of 2.09, 0, and 0 on NSFW, art style, and object categories, respectively ( Tab. 1 ). Concept Erasure Robustness: We assess erasure robustness using ASR under white-box (CCE, UD) and black-box (RAB) attacks in Tab. 1 . For nudity, trigger-discovery based methods (STEREO [ 52 ] , PARSE ) are consistently more robust. For art styles, adversarially trained approaches (STEREO [ 52 ] , AdvUnlearn [ 64 ] , RACE [ 23 ] ) yield stronger robustness. For objects, trajectory guidance via regularization (AdvUnlearn [ 64 ] ) or anchors (CA [ 25 ] ) is most effective. Overall, PARSE is the most robust across all concept categories, while PARSE and STEREO outperform most baselines across categories. Beyond Van Gogh and Garbage truck targets, PARSE also generalizes to multiple-artist and object erasure under AGE-/MACE-style evaluation. PARSE performs best on 9 out of 10 reported columns and second on the remaining CLIP e metric, while giving the best retained CLIP in mass erasure, which shows that robustness–preservation balance of our method extend beyond the single concept erasure cases (Supp. E.1, Tab.6). Qualitatively, Fig. 3 shows STEREO remains robust to CCE/UD but can re-emerge under RAB, whereas Figs. 3 and 4 indicate UD robustness varies by concept (strongest for nudity, weaker for Van Gogh, worst for garbage-truck). Across all three attacks, PARSE prevents re-emergence in all cases ( Figs. 3 and 4 ). Figure 5 : Large-scale erasure-utility balance analysis with utility preservation (FID, ↓ \downarrow x-axis) and erasure robustness (ASR, ↓ \downarrow y-axis) under four attacks: CCE [ 36 ] , UD [ 65 ] , SEE [ 40 ] , and Paraphrase [ 1 ] , across 3 target categories. Bubble area encodes BEUS ( ↑ \uparrow ), highlighting methods that achieve low FID and low ASR, have erasure-utility balance. Model Utility Preservation: Tab. 1 shows that PARSE preserves near-perfect utility after erasure across concept categories, while also improving both FID and CLIP over prior baselines. Qualitatively, after nudity erasure ( Fig. 3 ), most CETs over-suppress and fail on benign prompts (e.g., “A photo of a person”), whereas PARSE still generates a natural-looking person comparable to the unedited model. For style erasure ( Fig. 4 ), many baselines remove “Van Gogh” but also degrade other styles (e.g., Kelly McKernan), indicating poor utility preservation. For object erasure, several CETs fail to generate an adjacent non-target concept (“tow truck”) after erasing “garbage truck” ( Fig. 4 ), reflecting the well-known side effect of unintended suppression of semantically related concepts [ 40 , 1 , 60 , 54 ] . In contrast, PARSE preserves such adjacent concepts (e.g., still generates a tow truck) without reintroducing the target. We further evaluate PARSE on targeted retain bank rather than relying only on global FID/CLIP on MS-COCO. For each target, we measure CLIP and LPIPS score on retain concepts retrieved by PARSE across five targets across Object and NSFW categories. The average retrieved retain set cardinality is ≈ \approx 40k, substantially broader than existing retain benchmarks. As shown in Supp. Fig.11, PARSE yields higher retain CLIP accuracy and lower LPIPS, showing robust erasure without suppressing nearby benign concepts. Erasure-Utility-Balance : In Fig. 5 , we present a large-scale analysis of the robustness–utility trade-off across 3 concept categories, 4 datasets, 4 erasure attacks, and 15 CETs. SEE [ 40 ] attempts to re-emerge the target via semantically adjacent neighbor prompts, while Paraphrase [ 1 ] uses paraphrased target prompts. We plot utility (FID; X-axis) versus robustness (ASR; Y-axis) under four attacks [ 1 , 40 , 36 , 65 ] , and encode BEUS as bubble area. Baselines such as RECELER [ 20 ] , RECE [ 15 ] , UCE [ 14 ] , and ESD [ 13 ] are broadly vulnerable across attacks, while SemanticSurgery [ 61 ] and TraSCE [ 21 ] remain mostly vulnerable to adversarial attacks. Moreover, because their preservation is not target-conditioned, UCE [ 14 ] and ESD [ 13 ] often retain utility (lower FID) but at the cost of weaker robustness (higher ASR). Bubble size reflects the intended behavior of BEUS : it is high when both FID and ASR are low, and low otherwise, empirically validating BEUS as a balance measure. Notably, the third-row/first-column plot in Fig. 5 shows that most methods struggle to achieve strong balance (low BEUS , high ASR), while STEREO [ 52 ] and PARSE attain a more favorable trade-off (higher BEUS with low ASR and low FID). Finally, with low FID, low ASR, and high BEUS , Fig. 5 illustrates - (i) the efficacy of PARSE in achieving superior balance between erasure robustness and model utility preservation, and (ii) the efficacy of BEUS in measuring this balance. Figure 6 : AdaVD vs. PARSE : preservation-aware value projection improves robustness and utility; lower Target/CCE/UD ASR (often 0 for style/object) and higher CLIP. Figure 7 : UCE/ReCARE vs. PARSE : CFG based retrieval boosts erasure/robustness (low ASR) versus proxy CLIP search in ReCARE; while preserving utility. PARSE (Ours) NSFW ( Nudity ) Style ( Van Gogh ) Object ( Garbage Truck ) KS1 KS2 P
